1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,132
Book Two, Proposition Eight.

2
00:00:02,132 --> 00:00:16,990
If a segment be cut at random, four times the rectangle contained by the whole and one of the segments together with the square on the remaining segment is equal to the square described on the whole and the aforesaid segment as on one segment.

3
00:00:16,990 --> 00:00:20,170
Let the segment A B be cut at random at the point C.

4
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:26,901
Produce A B in a straight line to D, and cut off B D equal to C B by One Three.

5
00:00:26,901 --> 00:00:41,280
Describe the square A E F D on A D by One Forty-Six, draw the diagonal D E, and draw the double figure with parallels C H and B L to A E, and M N and O P to A D, by One Thirty-One.

6
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:49,393
Because C B equals B D, opposite sides in the parallelograms give G K equal to K N and Q R equal to R P.

7
00:00:49,393 --> 00:01:00,266
By One Thirty-Six and One Forty-Three, the four small corner squares D K, C K, G R, and R N are all equal to one another, and are quadruple of C K.

8
00:01:00,266 --> 00:01:08,772
In the same way, the four rectangles A G, M Q, Q L, and R F are all equal to one another, and are quadruple of A G.

9
00:01:08,772 --> 00:01:20,311
Together, these eight areas form the gnomon S T U, which is quadruple of the whole rectangle A K—that is, four times the rectangle contained by A B and B D.

10
00:01:20,311 --> 00:01:25,192
Add the inner square O H, which equals the square on A C, to both.

11
00:01:25,192 --> 00:01:30,074
The gnomon S T U and O H complete the whole square A E F D on A D.

12
00:01:30,074 --> 00:01:42,279
Since B D equals B C, four times the rectangle contained by A B and B C together with the square on A C equals the square described on A B and B C as on one segment.

13
00:01:42,279 --> 00:01:43,980
Which was to be proved.

14
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:50,993
In modern algebraic notation, if A B is a and B C is b, then A C is a minus b and A D is a plus b.

15
00:01:50,993 --> 00:02:03,911
Proposition Two Eight establishes the identity: four a b plus the square of a minus b equals the square of a plus b—a relation central to Apollonius's theory of the parabola and Euclid's Data.

16
00:02:03,911 --> 00:02:18,040
